#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/id #uuid "2230e2dd-e47a-5db3-bb07-f89d0396440b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6573" :question/problem "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t})-4u{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-5{u}^{′}{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}({u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^t \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0.\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u e^{-t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left( \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124287 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba3e0748-2a14-547e-b21e-c34251994a94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Differential Equation Satisfied by the Reduction of Order Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bd03341b-1190-51fa-b3f0-6fd921e3430c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6574" :question/problem "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})+9u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-6{u}^{′}-18u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{3x} \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5fc7fc-2f90-556e-a8ba-841ee4345c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123611} {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4f5584-8fc7-5337-ab56-9e4c5e615f17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123613} {:answer/id #uuid "2c3ceaf6-e888-5fb4-82cc-4ead9996c36b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123614} {:answer/id #uuid "3a307f38-89b4-5782-9efc-cba840fb9d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d996ab88-8a1c-5c7e-bf9f-eab5d48182f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{2}(x) = \\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123612} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a5512a1-ab4e-5f23-a650-68685dcdaff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ed6836b6-963c-5315-9c37-229f40227bb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64958" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c49977a9-ac11-5940-8fd1-97d5fb0341de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342546} {:answer/id #uuid "2c75840c-2d94-531b-8d9f-a96f1ae1a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342547} {:answer/id #uuid "cd102047-2d61-512e-918c-3838a762c61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342548} {:answer/id #uuid "dbbfab88-5e2c-5129-8fc9-51fe7cbf9b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342549} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe637e3-435a-5a7f-b032-f5a4f17c934a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c75840c-2d94-531b-8d9f-a96f1ae1a23d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342547} :answer-field/id #uuid "d47a58ef-ea6d-5f72-bea3-4ba11429fa49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342545}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16ff2884-3d19-5ae0-a798-f85100f86ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-64998" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e718a58-cfbc-5efe-9414-0ef1ab930662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342553} {:answer/id #uuid "648e0682-b58c-53a8-a699-c7ea9ca86128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342554} {:answer/id #uuid "54918d71-7b8c-52bf-ba39-c9e57a0e3dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342555} {:answer/id #uuid "6e54b5a5-5e25-5c12-a450-5d9b1393593f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342556} {:answer/id #uuid "5086bfa6-3589-54df-96f2-d31a64efb773" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54918d71-7b8c-52bf-ba39-c9e57a0e3dad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342555} :answer-field/id #uuid "c327756f-014f-5e94-9e1c-2ef1f0a0dc03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342552}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2edeb67d-5fd1-5efb-89bb-3d7d3065db3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65000" :question/problem "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." :question/worked-solution "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$"}]}
